{"items":[{"recordId":"teach-generated-4fede931d330df5e","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"In the source's Δ-Net terminology, annihilation is the reduction applicable when two equal replicators have matching port deltas at every port. The replicator nodes disappear and their corresponding ports reconnect.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-generated-e4dc860f89307587","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"common_knowledge","text":"A well-formed lambda translation converts an expression into lambda calculus, a notation built from variables, function definitions, and function applications, while preserving which function definition binds each variable. It avoids variable capture, meaning that substitution must not accidentally make a variable refer to a different enclosing function.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false}]},{"recordId":"teach-generated-dbc180a389e526e6","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"In the source's Δ-Net terminology, a replica is an outgoing copy of a shared value or agent produced so that another use can proceed. The replica carries level information associated with the port through which it leaves the replicator.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-generated-fc39155b2ba342e3","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"In the source's Δ-Net terminology, a port delta is the per-port value computed as the occurrence level minus the replicator level. Equal replicators can annihilate only when this value matches at every corresponding port.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-generated-8650ff183d0a118b","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"common_knowledge","text":"A global schedule is one plan that determines the order or timing of actions across an entire system, rather than separate plans for individual components. It is used to coordinate system-wide activity.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false}]},{"recordId":"teach-generated-3b9bd475b11ed6e9","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"common_knowledge","text":"Optimality means achieving the best possible result according to a stated objective while obeying the problem's constraints. A claim of optimality is meaningful only after the objective and admissible alternatives have been specified.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false}]},{"recordId":"teach-generated-e1c021b92c2eb4c2","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"In the source's Δ-Net terminology, a sharing context is the context created when several uses join around one shared value. A replicator records the level and per-port delta information needed to distribute that value consistently to those uses.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-generated-473d8b3af3720864","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"In the source's Δ-Net terminology, a variable-arity replicator joins the uses of one shared value, with the number of outgoing uses determining how many replicas it produces. It stores its level and the delta for each port so that each outgoing replica receives the appropriate level adjustment.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]}]}
